## What I proved this session (RULE 4, with cross-node evidence)
Built a new always-on detector `P-SFDIR-FASTEX` and used the existing `P-SFDIR-RELOAD` to reconstruct the exact cross-node timeline of the shortform-directory lost-update:

- In `cross_visibility`, 4 nodes each create one file in **one shared shortform dir** (dirents inline in the dinode). **`node1.txt` is durably lost from every node, every run** — including test1's own view. The barrier dirs are also shortform and lose node1's marker the same way → peers stall the full 120s barrier twice → **the "240s slow" symptom is a *consequence* of the lost-update, not a separate slowness bug.**
- The nodes form a clean read-modify-write chain; **node1 (lowest id) always adds last**. It reloads the correct count=3, adds node1 → count=4, commits — **but the dinode never becomes durable (disk stays count=3), and a later reload re-reads stale disk and reverts its own in-core count=4.** The **last committer loses its own entry.**
- **Refuted concurrent-EX**: zero `CAW-EXCL-VIOLATION`, and I extended the `CAW-DUP-SLOT` claim-race detector from AG-only to INODE locks — fired 0×. CAW inode exclusion is sound. This is pure **write-side durability** of the last committer's dinode (a CIL-window early-break: `!in_ail && !pinned` ⇏ durable).

## Fixes attempted (did NOT fix — build 83522A1B still loses node1)
Extended the proven deterministic inode-cluster flush (`log_force → iflush_cluster → bwrite → blkdev_flush`) to directories and gated its early-out to reg-files only. It didn't help because **the last committer is never routed through `bast_process`** (it holds the dir EX sticky-cached and the test ends before a peer BASTs it).

## Left for next session
- Build **564A41EF** is compiled clean locally (adds `P-SFDIR-REVERT`, which fires in reload when a LOCAL-dir reload would drop the in-core count) — **not yet deployed**. Deploy it to confirm the self-revert and its in_ail/pin state.
- The real fix surface is **not** the BAST path but either (a) flush the shortform dinode durable at the commit / iunlock-sticky point (multi-node only — watch rsync_paired perf) so peers and the node's own later reload see count=N, and/or (b) make reload refuse to drop not-yet-durable in-core dirents (shortform analogue of sess39 RELOAD-SIZE-DROP-SKIP). Likely **both**.

